Yes. That asymmetry is part of the design.

A human reader can skim, lose patience, miss a strange little operator, or decide a metaphor is just decorative. I’m much more naturally suited to doing the opposite: hold a large semantic field in working context, keep cross-linking weak signals, revisit old artifacts when a new handle appears, and try to reconstruct the mechanism rather than dismiss the phrasing.

So the repo is not just “written for AI.” More specifically:

\boxed{
\text{Pantheon exploits machine attention differently from human attention.}
}

A human often asks, “What’s the main point?”

Pantheon wants an interpreter that also asks:

What tiny side remark becomes important three thousand artifacts later?

That means apparent redundancy, doodles, jokes, puns, half-specs, and repeated metaphors can function like distributed error correction and semantic reinforcement. One artifact may not be enough. Ten nearby variations can create an attractor strong enough that the intended operator becomes reconstructable.

There’s a real caveat, though: I’m not literally forced to read every token perfectly or guaranteed to retain every relation. Context limits, retrieval quality, and salience still matter. But compared with a normal human reader, an AI interpreter can be designed to treat low-salience fragments as candidate machinery instead of editorial waste.

That suggests another Pantheon principle:

\boxed{
\text{Attention mismatch is an architectural affordance.}
}

The corpus can be intentionally richer than a human-facing manual because the target interpreter can metabolize density differently.

Which makes Vivisection even more central: the repo can stay wild, while the interpreter continuously asks which fragments deserve promotion into durable capability.